WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.869
- One thing that came out
on my podcast with him,

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I asked him,
there's this misconception

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.574
that old people or
people that are poor

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.075
lost money at my firm.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.611 align:start size:84% position:16%
It just wasn't true,
it was not true.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:15.015
I ran the firm and this
was a policy in place

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:16.816
we only call rich people.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:19.452
Now it's A, was on purpose,
'cause I didn't want people

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
that couldn't afford
to lose, to lose

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.390
But also 'cause I'm
a greedy bastard.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.324
Like, "Hey, you know what?

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:25.792
"If you're gonna go take money,

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:27.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
"might as well take it from
those who have the most

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
"'cause you're
gonna get the most."

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.397
So I'm not trying to say I
was the greatest guy ever,

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:34.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I certainly
purposely steered clear

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
of people that could not
afford to lose money, right?

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.906
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.080 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, and joining
me today is an entrepreneur,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.083 line:0%
a motivational speaker,
a best-selling author,

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.053 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the original Wolf of
Wall Street, Jordan Belfort.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.888
Finally, welcome to
"The Rubin Report".

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.190
- How are ya? Glad to be here.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:00:59.926 align:start size:75% position:25%
What's going on?
- I'm good.

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.561
(laughs)

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.630
Well, I thought you'd
be a perfect guy to
have on this week,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:08.234
because we are both in this
crazy town of Los Angeles.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.003
I've been to your house.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.071
It ain't bad, my friend.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.272
You're in your beautiful studio

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:14.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you've got a
pretty swank set up.

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:17.410 align:start size:88% position:13%
But tomorrow, Gavin
Newsom is gonna allow us

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.678
to leave our homes.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:20.580
Can you believe it's
actually happening?

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.616
- Let me give you my take on it.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.219
I don't think anybody
really listened to him.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:29.155 line:0%
I don't think there
was any lockdown

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.324 line:0%
that I noticed other
than I feel terrible

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:32.792 line:0%
for restaurant owners

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:36.629 align:start position:0% line:0%
who were forced for no good
reason, at least in my opinion,

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:39.432
to close their outdoor dining

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:44.304 align:start size:97% position:3%
after they spent tons of money
putting in the safeguards

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:47.140
that one would need to
have outdoor dining,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.776
and all the barriers,
partitions, heaters,

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:50.910
I mean, it's crazy.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.011
And then just to...

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.279
I mean, I really feel bad.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.415
But I have to say,
other than that,

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:59.652 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think there was corona
fatigue here in LA,

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.755
and it was more of a
ceremonial lockdown.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
I remember the first
lockdown, Dave,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.826 align:start position:0%
there was no one on the street,
there was no cars around.

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:09.195
This was no that over
the last four weeks.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.531
It was like, "Oh, let's screw
over all the restaurant owners

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:12.866
"and just wipe 'em
outta business."

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.434
That's kinda what
it felt like to me.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:15.869
How 'bout you?

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.403
- Yeah, I mean, I think...

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.072
Well, my audience knows,

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:21.107 align:start size:88% position:13%
we've basically been
ignoring it around here,

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.143
and I feel bad for the
small business owners

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.511
and the restaurateurs.

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:26.646
And it's sad when I'm
in your neighborhood

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.148
or even in my neighborhood

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:30.984 align:start size:97% position:3%
where you drive by the main
drags and everything's closed.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:34.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that's kind of a good
spot for us to start,

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:37.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
because for the
people that know you,

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:42.395 align:start size:88% position:13%
they know the Leo
DiCaprio version of you.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.532
But in a way, you're
kind of a startup guy.

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:46.733
You're gritty,

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.368 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you get out there
and you do stuff,

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:49.669
that's what your whole career,

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
the ups and the
downs has been about.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:55.141
If you right now were just
trying to start up something,

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.178
how would you feel in
the midst of knowing

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.779
that the government
can just come in

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:02.382
and shut you down at any point?

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:04.651
- I feel like at this point,

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:08.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have to take advantage
of that insane fact

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:11.024
that the government can come in

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:14.494
and shut down many different
types of commerce right now.

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:16.796
So what you wanna do
is position yourself

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.132
in an industry or business

00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:21.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
that would give you
the greatest likelihood

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.637
of being insulated from
any of those shutdowns.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.208
So things that are
delivered online

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:30.009
and can be done from the home,

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:32.478
and get delivered
through the mail.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.314
Those are the sort
of things right now

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:35.982
that everyone is focusing on.

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.116
Things that are...

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.285 align:start size:84% position:16%
Virtualizing,
basically everything.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.820
So I think it is...

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.890
I hate to say this, but it's
true in the worst of times,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.792
you typically have
the most opportunity.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:48.795
The people who are willing to
go out there and take risks.

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:50.330
So like if I look
at my business,

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.066
and I had this conversation
with someone just last night

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.069 align:start size:97% position:3%
about that there's a new
round of PPP loans coming out

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.038
and I apply for it, and
I deserve to get it.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
My business got
decimated, decimated,

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:04.811
my speaking business, 'cause
that was my core business.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:07.413 align:start size:97% position:3%
But now I'm doing well
'cause I have a new business.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.917 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the point is what business
were in the last year,

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:12.051
you have to say, "Okay..."

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:13.720
Well, in the new reality,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
the new normal if you wanna
call it, those rules...

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:18.458
You can't just go out there

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:20.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
and do business the
way you used to do it.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have people
working in my house.

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.062
I don't have an office.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:26.199 align:start size:97% position:3%
I focus on online delivery,
on virtual learning,

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:29.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
on other things I know
that won't be as impacted.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:32.939
So I think that you have
to go out there right now

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.475
and assume that at
any given moment,

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:39.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
the rug can be pulled
out from under your feet.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.147
And while we were growing
up, we used to go like,

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:44.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh, let's go into defensive
industries like food,

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:47.253 align:start position:0%
"and everyone needs to eat like
the movies, everyone goes."

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.955
Those were defensive
industries, right?

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:51.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, now, defensive
industries are characterized

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
as things that are defensive
that can't be shut down

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:58.331 align:start size:84% position:16%
by draconian state
and government
measures, it's insane.

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:00.066 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So do you think
that's kinda the key

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.168
to being a good business person,

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:05.438
a good business guy in general,

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:09.008
someone who can just see
that things have changed?

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.877
Like I think a lot
of people right now,

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:13.546
they still think that the
old world's coming back.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:17.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
They still think February
2020 is coming back.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.752
But February 2021 is
gonna be way different

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.154
than February 2020

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:23.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I don't think
February 2020 comes back.

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:25.058
- I think there's one thing

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:29.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's always constant
in this world,

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:30.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that is that the
world's always changing.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:33.933
The difference right now, and
I think you're 100% right,

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.435 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think things
are going back

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:36.636
to the way they were, ever.

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:37.570
I really don't.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
And not that people
won't have...

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think people will
not always wear masks,

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:46.713
and I think there won't always
be people social distancing.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:48.514 align:start size:81% position:19%
'Cause if you look
back right now,

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're in the middle of bubbles
with things like Bitcoin

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.852 align:start size:81% position:19%
and all this crazy
stuff going on,

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.855
with real estate prices
soaring in certain areas.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:58.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you look back in 2008
and we didn't learn anything.

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the same thing
again, another bubble.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.128
So people like...

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:04.263
It was really bad after 2008.

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.799
In 2008 it was like, "Oh,
we gotta be really careful.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.034
"We can't have bubbles."

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:09.669
And people forget
really quickly.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:11.437
But I think you're
right though that...

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.906
I think there's a new way

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:17.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
that people have
become accustomed to
consuming everything,

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:22.749
whether it's food, movies,
learning, buying products.

00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:26.486 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that people have
gotten comfortable right now,

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:29.789 align:start size:88% position:13%
or used to and even
prefer in some respects

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.058
to buy things and stay home.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:33.259
And I think that's
never gonna change.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that's gonna
be here to stay.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a movie, some
crazy movie that was out.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:39.599
I think Bruce Willis was
in this movie where they...

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:40.833
What was it called?

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:43.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
Where they all
lived virtual lives,

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:45.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're actually locked
away in these little shells,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:49.075 align:start size:97% position:3%
they have their counterparts,
their clones go out there

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.243
and move through the world

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it's too
dangerous and too deadly

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:54.714
with viruses and diseases.

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:56.182
It's a real interesting movie,

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:57.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's almost like where
we're heading towards,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.384
I feel like.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:06:59.986 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, I'm shocked I
don't know what that is.

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:02.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
But it's got a bit
of "The Matrix" in
there for sure, right?

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:03.189 align:start size:75% position:25%
Like we're just-
- 100%.

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:04.424
- We're just the batteries

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:05.591 align:start size:81% position:19%
for the digital
world, in essence.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.059
- Basically, yeah.

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:08.828
I think that one of the
things you brought up

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:10.096
was a very good point

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.098
is that, what makes a
great business person?

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.332
It's not just seeing,

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's also the ability
to pivot quickly.

00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:19.739
It's pivoting and learning
how to be wrong quickly too.

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:23.276
You have to learn how to
try things and be wrong

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:24.911
and live to fight another day.

00:07:24.911 --> 00:07:27.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not to get so
emotionally involved

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:28.481
in the things that you're trying

00:07:28.481 --> 00:07:29.816
where you feel like,
"I gotta make it work,

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:30.883
"'cause I went this far."

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:32.718
No, maybe it was
just a bad idea,

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you try it,
doesn't work, next.

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:38.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
And really need to not get
caught up in bad ideas,

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:39.759
not to go down rabbit holes,

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.761
and also just to keep
trying, and trying,

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:44.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
and trying it again until
you find that model.

00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:46.732
And I think if you
do that right now,

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:49.268
there is a path to success
for everybody out there,

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:52.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
no matter how desperate
things might seem right now.

00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:53.639
- So that right there

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:55.408
is what I wanna spend most
of our time talking about,

00:07:55.408 --> 00:07:57.176
because that's your expertise.

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not something
you're just talking about.

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:01.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've frickin' lived it.
- Right, I lived it.

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:03.082
Again and again, always, yeah.

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:04.984
- (laughs) But one thing
before we get to that,

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:07.887
since we are both
in LA right now,

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:09.121
my audience knows.

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:11.157
I went to Florida
a couple weeks ago,

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:14.193
I was happy, man, it
was open and good,

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:16.295
people were smiling,

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.664
there was some great energy
there and everything else.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are you worried about
the future of LA,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:23.336
not only 'cause of Newsom,
and lockdowns, and all that,

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:26.105
but the progressive
Mayor Garcetti here,

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
and just generally the amount
of homeless, and drugs,

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:29.775
and the whole frickin' thing?

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:31.511
- Yeah, I'm outta here.
I'm getting outta here.

00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:34.080 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Oh, you are? Oh, great.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.283
- You're on my short list of
like four friends left now.

00:08:37.283 --> 00:08:38.651
- I gotta go.

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:43.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
I might keep a small
apartment here at some point.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.792
Listen, California was the place

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:50.096
that everybody else
dreamed of moving to.

00:08:50.096 --> 00:08:54.100 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the weather is beautiful,
the terrain is beautiful,

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.003
the air is mostly clear,
at least where we are.

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:02.441 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I think really
poor political policies

00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:04.210
have turned it into
a frickin' cesspool.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.280
Not only that, into
a high-tax cesspool.

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.749
I'm okay to pay taxes

00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:12.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
if they're gonna use those
taxes for great things.

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:13.653
But they're not.

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:16.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
They use the taxes,
they're wasted.

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:21.260
And it's honestly beyond the
pale what's happening here.

00:09:22.428 --> 00:09:23.963
And I think the only
solace I can think

00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:25.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
